#
# The boot command for this project's own board, served by its TFTP server
# and fetched by U-Boot's cadr_net when the card's uEnv.txt names a server.
# Edit here, copy to /srv/tftp/cora-z7-07s, reset the board; the card is
# never touched.  A card without a server named on it never reads this file:
# it boots the same five files from the card (cadr_cora.env's cadr_card).
#
# EVERY PATH HERE IS UNDER cora-z7-07s/, AND THAT IS THE RULE FOR EVERY
# BOARD.  One TFTP server serves more than one board here, and every board's
# five files carry the same five names, so a flat server root would hand this
# board the Arty's bitstream --- a bitstream for the wrong part, which
# configures nothing and says nothing about why.  A board's served set
# therefore sits in a directory named as its directory under `boards/` is.
# The names below are relative to the server's root and not to this file, so
# a copy of this file anywhere on that server still fetches the right five.
#
# WHAT IT DOES.  The CADR goes into the fabric first: `fpga loadb` configures
# it with cadr.bit and the machine is running its boot PROM out of DDR3
# before the kernel is fetched.  Then the tree, the kernel and the root
# filesystem --- a zImage, a U-Boot ramdisk image (rootfs.cpio.uboot, the
# initramfs) and a raw tree --- and cadr_bootz, which is the same last step
# the card path takes: `bootz` with all three and the built-in bootargs.  The
# addresses are U-Boot's environment's, set and explained in cadr.env.
#
# WHAT IS NOT ON THE COMMAND LINE, against the stepping stone's.  No
# `mem=384M`: the tree's reserved-memory node, `no-map`, is what keeps this
# kernel off the CADR's region --- measured on the board 10 September, System
# RAM 00000000-17ffffff from the node alone.  No `cma=32M`: nothing here
# allocates from CMA.  No `uio_pdrv_genirq.of_id`: there are no PL
# peripherals in the tree.  `earlycon` with no argument takes the console
# from the tree's stdout-path.
#
# fdt_high and initrd_high are left unset: U-Boot relocates the tree and the
# ramdisk into memory it allocates below its own, and the reserved-memory
# node in its tree keeps that below the CADR's region.
# AND THE FAULT BITSTREAM WHEN THE CADR'S CANNOT BE LOADED, as on the card
# path (cadr_fabric_card in U-Boot's own environment): fault.bit from the same
# directory, and the boot goes on, so that Linux says what happened.
cadr_fabric_net=if tftpboot ${cadr_bit_addr} cora-z7-07s/cadr.bit && fpga loadb 0 ${cadr_bit_addr} ${filesize}; then echo "cadr: the CADR bitstream is loaded"; else echo "cadr: THE CADR BITSTREAM DID NOT LOAD; loading the fault bitstream, whose lamps all blink"; tftpboot ${cadr_bit_addr} cora-z7-07s/fault.bit && fpga loadb 0 ${cadr_bit_addr} ${filesize}; fi
netcmd=run cadr_fabric_net && tftpboot ${cadr_fdt_addr} cora-z7-07s/zynq-cora-z7-07s.dtb && tftpboot ${cadr_kernel_addr} cora-z7-07s/zImage && tftpboot ${cadr_ramdisk_addr} cora-z7-07s/rootfs.cpio.uboot && run cadr_bootz
